.TH RNNOISE_STREAM 1 "November 2026" "rnnoise" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rnnoise_stream \- streaming RNNoise denoiser with VAD timestamps
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rnnoise_stream
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIINPUT\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rnnoise_stream
reads mono or multichannel WAV or raw PCM, denoises it with RNNoise in
10 ms (480 sample) frames, and writes denoised audio while streaming.
The reader, inference worker and writer run in separate threads connected
by bounded queues, so memory use does not grow with the input length.
.PP
Samples are downmixed to mono before denoising.  The denoised mono signal is
written to all channels of the output.  Audio not sampled at 48 kHz is
resampled to 48 kHz for inference and back to the original rate for output.
The output always uses the input sample rate and channel count.
.PP
VAD probabilities are written as CSV with one row per 10 ms inference frame.
The first output audio frame and the first VAD row both start at time zero.
The worker compensates for RNNoise's one-frame synthesis delay before writing.
.PP
Use
.B \-
for stdin or stdout.  A WAV header written to a non-seekable pipe uses
0xffffffff for the RIFF and data sizes; a regular output file is patched with
the final sizes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-f, \-\-input-format " "auto | wav | raw"
Select input format.  The default is
.BR auto ,
which recognizes RIFF/WAVE and treats everything else as raw PCM.
.TP
.BI "\-F, \-\-output-format " "auto | wav | raw"
Select output format.  With
.BR auto ,
stdin/stdout inherits the input format and a path ending in
.B .wav
is WAV; other paths are raw.
.TP
.BI "\-r, \-\-sample-rate " HZ
Raw input sample rate.  Default: 48000.
.TP
.BI "\-c, \-\-channels " N
Raw input channel count.  Default: 1.
.TP
.BI "\-\-sample-format " FORMAT
Raw input sample format.  Default: s16le.  Valid values are u8, s16le, s16be,
s24le, s24be, s32le, s32be, f32le and f32be.
.TP
.BI "\-\-output-sample-format " FORMAT
Raw output sample format.  Default: the input sample format.
.TP
.BI "\-\-vad " FILE
Write the VAD CSV to FILE, or to stdout when FILE is
.BR \- .
The default is OUTPUT.vad.csv, or INPUT.vad.csv when the audio is written to
stdout.  When both audio streams are stdin/stdout this option is required.
.TP
.BI "\-\-model " FILE
Load a custom RNNoise model.
.TP
.BI "\-q, \-\-queue-frames " N
Maximum number of frames in each bounded thread queue.  Default: 32.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Do not print the startup summary to stderr.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show usage information.
.SH OUTPUT
The VAD file starts with:
.PP
.nf
time_seconds,vad_probability
0.000000,0.000123456
0.010000,0.000234567
.fi
.PP
Each row corresponds to the audio frame beginning at that timestamp.  Rows are
emitted in order even though reading, inference and writing are concurrent.
.SH EXAMPLES
Denoise a WAV file and keep a separate VAD CSV:
.PP
.nf
rnnoise_stream input.wav output.wav --vad output.vad.csv
.fi
.PP
Process raw 44.1 kHz stereo PCM from a pipe:
.PP
.nf
cat input.pcm | rnnoise_stream - - \\
  --sample-format s16le --sample-rate 44100 --channels 2 \\
  --output-format raw --output-sample-format s16le --vad vad.csv > out.pcm
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success.  A nonzero status is returned for invalid arguments,
unsupported PCM formats, truncated samples, I/O errors, or if the writer cannot
produce exactly the original number of audio frames.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rnnoise_demo (1)
